-- title:
--   Residues modulo lattices, symmetry sets $\mathrm{Sym}_G$, $\widehat{\mathrm{Sym}}$ and barycentric spanners
-- statement:
--   **Lattices and residues.** A lattice $\Lambda\subseteq\mathbb Z^d$ is an additive subgroup; it is **full-rank** (full-dimensional) when it has finite index in $\mathbb Z^d$, and then its determinant $\det(\Lambda)$ is that index. For $A\subseteq\mathbb Z^d$, $A\bmod\Lambda$ is the set of residue classes represented by vectors of $A$, a subset of the finite group $Z_\Lambda=\mathbb Z^d\bmod\Lambda$. $L(A)=\mathrm{span}_{\mathbb Z}(A)$ is the lattice generated by $A$.
--
--   **Symmetry sets (Theorem 1.5, Definition 6.1).** For a subset $X$ of an abelian group $G$,
--   $$\mathrm{Sym}_G(X)=\{g\in G: X+g=X\},$$
--   a subgroup of $G$. For $A\subseteq\mathbb Z^d$ and a full-rank $\Lambda$, $\mathrm{Sym}_{Z_\Lambda}(A):=\mathrm{Sym}_{Z_\Lambda}(A\bmod\Lambda)$, and the corresponding lattice is $\widehat{\mathrm{Sym}}_{Z_\Lambda}(A)=\{g\in\mathbb Z^d: g\bmod\Lambda\in\mathrm{Sym}_{Z_\Lambda}(A)\}$.
--
--   **Barycentric spanner (Definition 6.5).** For $A\subseteq\mathbb R^d$, $d$ linearly independent vectors $b_1,\dots,b_d\in A$ form a barycentric spanner of $A$ if
--   $$A\subseteq\Big\{\sum_{i=1}^d x_ib_i: |x_i|\le1\Big\}.$$
--
--   These notions carry the additive-combinatorics part of the mission (Kneser's theorem, Corollary 6.2, Lemmas 6.3–6.7, Theorem 1.3).
--
--   **Formalization Note** $\mathbb Z^d$ is `Fin d → ℤ`; $A\bmod\Lambda$ is the image of $A$ in the quotient group; $\mathrm{Sym}_G(X)$ is the stabilizer of $X$ under translation (`AddAction.stabilizer`), which in an abelian group is $\{g: X+g=X\}$. The spanner is an indexed linearly independent family in $A$; such a family is injective, so it is a set of $d$ vectors. The published `StochLinOpt_ConfBall2_IsBarycentricSpanner` omits linear independence and is not reused.
-- source:
--   Lin Chen, Tingwei Hu, Jiayi Lian, Yuchen Mao, Guochuan Zhang, Faster Approximation Schemes for Scheduling on Related Machines, STOC 2027 submission #796 (October 2026), source.pdf SHA-256 5d7d0135eed9cbe942c80598e2bb07d8c56ab67aec0d6e8486a067237654f7f3, Theorem 1.5 p.5; §6.1 p.18 lines 651–653; Definition 6.1 p.19; Definition 6.5 p.19; p.3 lines 101–103

import Mathlib

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace RelatedSchedFPTAS796.Main

/-!
# Residues modulo lattices, symmetry sets and barycentric spanners

Chen, Hu, Lian, Mao, Zhang, *Faster Approximation Schemes for Scheduling on Related Machines*,
STOC 2027 submission #796 (October 2026): Theorem 1.5 p.5 (`Sym_G(S) := {g ∈ G : S + g = S}`),
§6.1 p.18 lines 651–653 (residues), Definition 6.1 p.19 (symmetry sets), Definition 6.5 p.19
(barycentric spanner), and the notation of p.3 lines 101–103 (`L(A) = span_ℤ(A)`, `det(Z)`).

**Formalization Note.**
* `ℤ^d` is `Fin d → ℤ`. A lattice in `ℤ^d` is an additive subgroup; it is **full-rank**
  (full-dimensional) when it has finite index, and then its determinant `det(Λ)` equals its index
  `[ℤ^d : Λ]` (the absolute determinant of any basis). Statements use `Λ.FiniteIndex` and `Λ.index`.
* `A mod Λ`, the set of residue classes represented by `A`, is the image of `A` in `ℤ^d ⧸ Λ`;
  `Z_Λ = ℤ^d mod Λ` is the quotient group itself.
* `Sym_G(X)` is the stabilizer of `X` under translation, `{g : g + X = X}` (`g +ᵥ X`), which in an
  abelian group is `{g : X + g = X}`; it is a subgroup.
-/

open Pointwise

variable {d : ℕ}

/-- `A mod Λ`: the residue classes modulo `Λ` represented by vectors of `A`. -/
def modL (Λ : AddSubgroup (Fin d → ℤ)) (A : Set (Fin d → ℤ)) : Set ((Fin d → ℤ) ⧸ Λ) :=
  (QuotientAddGroup.mk : (Fin d → ℤ) → (Fin d → ℤ) ⧸ Λ) '' A

/-- **Theorem 1.5 / Definition 6.1.** The symmetry set `Sym_G(X) = {g ∈ G : X + g = X}` of a subset
`X` of an abelian group `G`, as the subgroup stabilizing `X` under translation. -/
def symG {G : Type*} [AddCommGroup G] (X : Set G) : AddSubgroup G :=
  AddAction.stabilizer G X

/-- **Definition 6.1.** `Sym_{Z_Λ}(A) := Sym_{Z_Λ}(A mod Λ)`, a subgroup of `Z_Λ = ℤ^d mod Λ`. -/
def symZ (Λ : AddSubgroup (Fin d → ℤ)) (A : Set (Fin d → ℤ)) : AddSubgroup ((Fin d → ℤ) ⧸ Λ) :=
  symG (modL Λ A)

/-- **Definition 6.1.** `Ŝym_{Z_Λ}(A) := {g ∈ ℤ^d : g mod Λ ∈ Sym_{Z_Λ}(A)}`, the corresponding
lattice in `ℤ^d`. -/
def symHat (Λ : AddSubgroup (Fin d → ℤ)) (A : Set (Fin d → ℤ)) : AddSubgroup (Fin d → ℤ) :=
  (symZ Λ A).comap (QuotientAddGroup.mk' Λ)

/-- `L(A) = span_ℤ(A)`, the lattice generated by `A`. -/
def latticeGen (A : Set (Fin d → ℤ)) : AddSubgroup (Fin d → ℤ) := AddSubgroup.closure A

/-- The coordinate embedding `ℤ^d → ℝ^d`. -/
def toReal (v : Fin d → ℤ) : Fin d → ℝ := fun i => (v i : ℝ)

/-- **Definition 6.5** (p.19). For `A ⊆ ℝ^d`, the `d` vectors `b_1, …, b_d` form a barycentric
spanner of `A` when they are linearly independent, lie in `A`, and
`A ⊆ {∑_i x_i b_i : |x_i| ≤ 1}`. (A linearly independent family is injective, so `{b_1, …, b_d}`
is a set of `d` vectors.) -/
def IsBarycentricSpanner (A : Set (Fin d → ℝ)) (b : Fin d → Fin d → ℝ) : Prop :=
  LinearIndependent ℝ b ∧ (∀ i, b i ∈ A) ∧
    ∀ a ∈ A, ∃ x : Fin d → ℝ, (∀ i, |x i| ≤ 1) ∧ a = ∑ i, x i • b i

end RelatedSchedFPTAS796.Main


